const a=[{id:"proj-architecture",q:"Walk me through your current project's architecture.",category:"project",principles:["Dive Deep","Ownership","Customer Obsession"],listeningFor:"Whether you understand the whole system and why it is built that way, not just your tickets. They want business purpose, scale, a clear data flow, your exact role, and trade-offs you can defend.",example:{situation:"I am a data engineer at an e-commerce marketplace with about 40,000 active sellers. My team owns the platform behind seller payouts and finance reporting, which ingests about 1.2 TB of new data a day from 35 Postgres tables, Kafka order events and partner files.",task:"I own the orders and payments domain end to end: 14 Airflow DAGs, the silver and gold models and their data-quality checks. The hard requirement is reconciled payout numbers in Redshift by 7 AM IST every day.",action:"Data lands three ways: AWS DMS streams CDC from Postgres into S3, a Kafka Connect S3 sink writes order events every 5 minutes, and partners push files to an AWS Transfer Family SFTP endpoint. I run Spark on EMR to MERGE the CDC into Iceberg silver tables partitioned by day. I chose Iceberg over plain Parquet because we need updates and deletes, and Athena can read the same tables. Gold tables are star schemas loaded into Redshift with COPY. Airflow on MWAA orchestrates everything, and a reconciliation against the payments ledger must pass before anything is published.",result:"The main batch runs in about 70 minutes on a 12-node cluster with spot task nodes and costs roughly Rs 3 lakh a month. We have missed the 7 AM SLA twice in six months, both from upstream outages. I can go deeper on the CDC merge or the reconciliation if useful."},tips:["Lead with what the data is for and how much there is before naming any tool.","Separate the team's system from your part of it in one clear sentence.","Keep a 5-6 box diagram in your head (sources, ingestion, storage, processing, serving, orchestration) and walk it left to right.","End by offering two places to go deeper, so you steer the conversation to your strongest material."],followUps:["Why Iceberg rather than Delta Lake, Hudi or plain Parquet?","What happens to a published gold table when a CDC delete arrives for an old order?","If the DMS task falls 3 hours behind, what breaks and how do you find out?","What would break first if volume grew 10x?"],redFlags:["Listing tools without saying what the data is for or how much of it there is.","Not knowing the volume, SLA, cluster size or rough cost of your own system.","Describing the whole platform with 'we' and never saying which part is yours."]},{id:"proj-hardest-problem",q:"What is the hardest technical problem you have solved?",category:"project",principles:["Dive Deep","Deliver Results","Frugality"],listeningFor:"Real technical depth: how you diagnosed the problem with evidence, the options you weighed, and a fix you clearly understand. Vague 'I optimised it' answers fail.",example:{situation:"Our daily seller-settlement Spark job on EMR joined about 900 million transactions with seller and fee tables. Over six months its runtime crept from 1.5 hours to over 4 hours, and it breached the 7 AM finance SLA about twice a week.",task:"I was asked to bring it back under 90 minutes without doubling the cluster, which would have added about Rs 1.5 lakh a month.",action:"I started in the Spark UI instead of guessing. One stage had 199 tasks finishing in under a minute and one task running for 2.5 hours, spilling 180 GB to disk. Counting rows by seller_id showed one aggregator seller had grown to 31% of all rows. AQE skew handling was on but did not help, because the skew sat in an aggregation before the join. I salted that key into 32 buckets, did a two-stage aggregation, and replicated the matching seller rows across the salts for the join. I also broadcast the 40 MB fee table to remove a shuffle, and wrote a unit test with a synthetic skewed dataset so the logic could not silently regress.",result:"Runtime dropped from 4 h 10 min to 52 minutes on the same cluster, with no SLA breaches since. I added an alert when any key exceeds 10% of a stage's rows, and three months later it caught a second skewed key before it caused a delay."},tips:["Pick a problem where you did the diagnosis yourself, so you can survive four levels of 'why'.","Show the evidence trail: what you measured, what it told you, what you ruled out.","Mention at least one option you rejected and why."],followUps:["Why didn't Adaptive Query Execution fix the skew on its own?","How did you choose 32 salt buckets?","What did the Spark UI show after the fix?","How would you solve the same problem if the skewed key were unknown in advance?"],redFlags:["A problem that was solved by 'adding more nodes' with no diagnosis.","Not being able to explain the fix at the level of stages, shuffles or partitions.","Picking a problem someone else solved and you watched."]},{id:"proj-redesign",q:"If you could redesign one pipeline you built, which one would it be and what would you change?",category:"project",principles:["Are Right, A Lot","Ownership","Think Big"],listeningFor:"Self-awareness and judgement. They want a real weakness, why it made sense at the time, and a better design with honest trade-offs. 'Nothing' is the worst answer.",example:{situation:"In my first year I built the pipeline that copies our orders and customer tables to the analytics lake. To ship fast, it took a full nightly snapshot of the 300 GB orders table from a Postgres read replica and overwrote the S3 copy.",task:"Two years later I still owned it and the table had grown to 1.4 TB. The job took 3 hours, loaded the replica heavily, and could not show intra-day changes or hard deletes.",action:"I wrote a one-page proposal comparing three options: parallelising the snapshot, watermark-based incremental loads on updated_at, and log-based CDC. I ruled out watermarks because about 4% of updates in our app did not touch updated_at, and deletes would be invisible. I proposed Debezium CDC into Kafka, then a Spark job running MERGE INTO an Iceberg table every 30 minutes, with a weekly full comparison of counts and sums to catch drift. I prototyped it on the two largest tables and measured replica load and merge time before asking for the rollout.",result:"The prototype replaced the 3-hour extract with 12-minute merges, and night-time replica CPU dropped from 85% to under 20%. I am rolling it out table by table, 9 of 22 done. If I started again I would design for incremental loads from day one, because the full-overwrite shortcut cost far more to undo than it saved."},tips:["Choose a design flaw, not a bug, so you can talk about trade-offs.","Explain why the original choice was reasonable at the time, without blaming anyone.","Best answers show you have already acted on it: a proposal, a prototype, a migration."],followUps:["How do you handle the initial snapshot plus the CDC stream without gaps or duplicates?","What does the weekly reconciliation do when it finds drift?","How do you handle compaction and small files with 30-minute merges?"],redFlags:["'I would not change anything, it was well designed.'","Blaming the previous team or the deadline without any lesson.","Proposing a fashionable tool without saying what problem it solves."]},{id:"proj-proudest",q:"Tell me about the project you are most proud of.",category:"project",principles:["Invent and Simplify","Think Big","Deliver Results"],listeningFor:"What you value and the scope you can handle. A strong answer has clear personal ownership, a simplification or idea that came from you, and impact beyond your own tickets.",example:{situation:"When I joined a fintech lending startup, every new source table meant a new hand-written Glue job. There were 45 of them, mostly copy-pasted, onboarding a table took 3-4 days, and they often broke on schema changes.",task:"I volunteered to build a framework so a new source could be onboarded in hours instead of days.",action:"I listed what actually varied across the 45 jobs: connection, primary key, incremental column, partition column, PII columns and schedule. Everything else was identical. I wrote one PySpark ingestion job driven by a YAML config per table, with schema-drift handling (new columns added automatically, type changes sent to a quarantine path), PII hashing, row-count checks, and Airflow DAGs generated from the configs. I migrated the 5 most painful jobs first to prove it, wrote docs and a template, and paired with two teammates while they moved the rest.",result:"Onboarding went from 3-4 days to about 2 hours, and we added 60 more tables over two quarters with no extra headcount. Failures from schema changes dropped from about 8 a month to 1, and two other teams adopted the framework. The lesson I took away was that the biggest win was the code I deleted."},tips:["Pick something where the idea, not just the execution, came from you.","Quantify both the direct result and the reach (other teams, tables onboarded).","Be ready to explain the design of the framework in detail."],followUps:["How does the framework handle a column type change from int to string?","What did you deliberately leave out of the config, and why?","How did you convince the team to migrate their jobs to it?"],redFlags:["A project with no measurable result.","Taking credit for a team-wide effort without naming your part."]},{id:"proj-tech-choice",q:"Tell me about a significant technical decision on your project. What alternatives did you consider?",category:"project",principles:["Are Right, A Lot","Customer Obsession","Bias for Action"],listeningFor:"That you start from requirements, compare real alternatives on criteria that matter (latency, cost, team skills, operations), and document the decision. Choosing by hype is a red flag.",example:{situation:"At a food-delivery company, city operations teams wanted a live view of delayed orders per zone. Our data was T+1 from a nightly batch, and peak load was about 4,000 order events a second at dinner time.",task:"I was asked to design the pipeline and recommend a stack. Once I pinned ops down, the real requirement was data under 2 minutes old with correct counts per zone.",action:"I compared three options. Flink had the lowest latency, but nobody on the team knew it and it meant a new cluster to run. Kinesis with Lambda was cheap, but windowed state with late events would be painful. Spark Structured Streaming on our existing EMR setup with 1-minute micro-batches met the 2-minute need, reused our Spark skills, and handled late events with a 10-minute watermark. I wrote a short decision doc with these trade-offs and the condition that would make us revisit it, a sub-10-second requirement. I load-tested at 3x peak by replaying Kafka data before go-live.",result:"We launched in 5 weeks with 60-90 seconds end-to-end latency at peak. Ops use it to reassign riders, and zone managers reported late-order escalations down about 18% in two months. A year later the decision doc ended a debate about switching to Flink, because the revisit condition had not been met."},tips:["Start with the requirement you clarified, especially if it differed from the first ask.","Compare on 3-4 named criteria, including team skills and operational cost.","Say what would make you change the decision. It shows judgement, not attachment."],followUps:["How did you choose the 10-minute watermark? What happens to events later than that?","How do you guarantee correct counts if the streaming job restarts?","What would you change if ops later needed 5-second latency?"],redFlags:["Choosing a tool because it is popular or because you wanted to learn it.","No alternatives considered.","Not knowing the latency or throughput numbers the design was built for."]},{id:"proj-data-quality",q:"How do you make sure the data your pipelines produce is correct? Give me a real example.",category:"project",principles:["Insist on the Highest Standards","Dive Deep","Ownership"],listeningFor:"A concrete quality strategy you built or used: which checks, at which layer, what happens when they fail, and evidence that it caught real problems. 'We test our code' is not enough.",example:{situation:"At a payments company, our gold tables fed daily merchant settlement reports. Twice in one quarter, a silent upstream change produced wrong numbers that finance found before we did.",task:"I took on building a data-quality layer so we caught problems before publishing, not after a stakeholder complained.",action:"I ranked our 30 gold tables by impact and started with the 8 that touched money. For each I added three levels of checks: schema and null checks on ingestion, row-count and freshness checks against the previous 14 days with a tolerance, and a business reconciliation comparing daily totals with the payments ledger to the rupee. I implemented them with Deequ inside the Spark jobs, wrote results to a table, and changed the Airflow DAGs so a failed critical check blocked publishing and paged on-call, while warnings went to Slack. I spent two weeks tuning thresholds to cut false alarms.",result:"Over the next six months the checks caught 11 issues before publishing, including an upstream amount field that silently switched from paise to rupees. Data issues reported by finance went from 2 a quarter to 0, and the reconciliation dashboard became part of finance's month-end close."},tips:["Name the check types and the layer they run at, not just the tool.","Say what happens on failure: block, quarantine, warn, page.","Give at least one real issue the checks caught."],followUps:["How did you choose tolerances without creating alert fatigue?","What do you do when a critical check fails at 5 AM and the SLA is 7 AM?","Which checks would you add for a streaming pipeline?"],redFlags:["Only unit tests on code, with nothing checking the data itself.","Checks that only alert and never block bad data."]},{id:"own-beyond-role",q:"Tell me about a time you took ownership of a problem that was outside your area of responsibility.",category:"ownership",principles:["Ownership","Dive Deep","Earn Trust"],listeningFor:"That you act on problems you notice even when they are not yours, while respecting the actual owners. They want initiative plus collaboration, not going rogue.",example:{situation:"Our clickstream pipeline consumed app events from a Kafka topic owned by the mobile backend team. I noticed our daily active-user numbers had been about 3% lower than product's SDK numbers for weeks.",task:"It was not my code, since our pipeline processed exactly what arrived. But product managers were deciding things on our numbers, so I decided to find the cause.",action:"I compared raw event counts by app version and found the gap was almost all from one Android release. Reading the producer code in the backend team's repo, I saw that release batched events into bigger payloads, some over the 1 MB default request size, and the producer callback logged the RecordTooLargeException and moved on. I wrote up the evidence with sample payloads and took it to their tech lead rather than just filing a ticket. They were busy with a release, so I offered to make the change myself: compression, splitting oversized batches, and a metric on failed sends. They reviewed and merged it.",result:"The gap fell to under 0.2% within a week, and the failed-send metric is now on their dashboard. Product corrected their DAU trend with our numbers. The backend team later asked me to review their next producer change, which made the two teams much easier to work across."},tips:["Show you involved the real owner rather than silently changing their system.","Explain why it mattered to a customer, not just that it was technically interesting.","End with a lasting change, such as a metric, process or relationship."],followUps:["What would you have done if the backend team had said no?","How did you prioritise this against your own work?","How did you confirm the fix closed the whole gap?"],redFlags:["Changing another team's production system without their approval.","Stopping at 'I raised a ticket' and never following up."]},{id:"own-incident",q:"Tell me about a time you saw a difficult problem through to the end.",category:"ownership",principles:["Ownership","Deliver Results","Customer Obsession"],listeningFor:"That you fix the immediate impact and then the root cause, and that you communicate along the way. Stopping at 'I reran the job' shows a lack of ownership.",example:{situation:"On the last working day before month-end close, the Redshift load for our finance gold tables started failing at 2 AM while I was on call. Finance needed the numbers by 9 AM to close the books for about 600 cost centres.",task:"I had to get correct data loaded in time, then make sure it could not happen again.",action:"STL_LOAD_ERRORS showed 214 rows where a vendor-name field was longer than the varchar column, because upstream had started putting long descriptions in it. Instead of widening the column in a panic, I loaded all rows with that field truncated, kept the full originals in a quarantine table, and checked that totals matched the source to the rupee, since the amount columns were unaffected. At 6 AM I messaged the finance lead with what happened and what was and was not in the data. After close, I traced the cause to an unannounced upstream change, agreed a schema contract with that team, added a pre-load Spark check against Redshift column widths, and wrote the runbook.",result:"Finance closed on time with fully reconciled totals, and the 214 names were corrected the same day. We had no further load failures from width changes in the next year, and the pre-load check is now part of our template for 40 tables."},tips:["Split your answer into the immediate fix and the permanent fix.","Show how you communicated with the affected stakeholder during the incident.","Quantify both the business stake (deadline, users) and the long-term result."],followUps:["Why not just widen the column at 3 AM?","What exactly is in the schema contract with the upstream team?","How would you have handled it if the amount columns had been affected?"],redFlags:["The story ends when the job turns green.","Silently changing data without telling consumers what changed."]},{id:"own-tech-debt",q:"Describe a time you fixed a long-standing problem that nobody had asked you to fix.",category:"ownership",principles:["Ownership","Insist on the Highest Standards","Strive to be Earth's Best Employer"],listeningFor:"Long-term thinking: noticing a chronic problem, making the case for time to fix it, and leaving the team better off. Bonus if you measured before and after.",example:{situation:"Our Airflow setup sent around 120 alerts a week to a shared Slack channel. Most were transient failures that succeeded on retry, so people had stopped reading them, and a real failure in a revenue pipeline went unnoticed for 9 hours.",task:"Nobody owned alerting. After that miss, I asked my manager for 20% of my time for a month to fix it.",action:"I exported three months of alert history and categorised every alert by DAG, cause and whether a human had to act. 70% were source timeouts fixed by a retry, so I added retries with exponential backoff and alerted only on final failure. I introduced two severities: pipelines with an SLA paged on-call through PagerDuty, and everything else went into a daily digest. I added SLA-miss callbacks to the 15 DAGs that fed external stakeholders and wrote a one-page runbook for each critical DAG. I reviewed the severity list with the team so everyone agreed what counted as critical.",result:"Alerts fell from about 120 a week to 15, and every page now needs action. Time to detect a real failure on critical pipelines went from hours to under 10 minutes, with no unnoticed failures in the next two quarters. A runbook is now a required part of every new DAG's review, and on-call weeks are noticeably calmer."},tips:["Start with the cost of the problem in concrete terms (an incident, hours lost).","Show how you got time and agreement, not just that you did it alone.","Measure before and after."],followUps:["How did you decide which DAGs were critical?","What did you do about the root cause of the source timeouts?","How do you stop the alert count creeping back up?"],redFlags:["Doing large unplanned work without telling your manager.","No measurable improvement."]},{id:"fail-prod-mistake",q:"Tell me about a time you made a mistake that impacted production.",category:"failure",principles:["Earn Trust","Ownership","Insist on the Highest Standards"],listeningFor:"Whether you own mistakes plainly, contain the damage fast, communicate early, and put guards in place so it cannot recur. They are testing honesty more than the mistake itself.",example:{situation:"I was backfilling one day of corrected data into a 2 TB events table on S3, partitioned by event_date and used by about 20 analysts and two ML models.",task:"The fix was small and I wanted it done before the morning reports, so I ran the backfill myself from a notebook.",action:"The job wrote with mode overwrite and partitionBy, but Spark's partitionOverwriteMode was at its default, static, so instead of replacing one partition it deleted every partition and wrote only that day. The row-count alert fired within 15 minutes. I told my lead immediately and posted in the analysts' channel not to use the table. S3 versioning was on, so I wrote a script to restore the previous object versions under the deleted prefixes, checked counts per partition against the previous day's data-quality results, and had the table back in about 2 hours. Then I wrote a blameless post-mortem that stated my mistake plainly.",result:"Morning reports were about 90 minutes late and no data was lost. From the post-mortem I made dynamic partition overwrite the cluster default, added a guard in our shared write utility that refuses a full overwrite of a partitioned table without an explicit flag, and moved backfills to a reviewed Airflow DAG. I now dry-run every backfill on a copy first."},tips:["Pick a real mistake that was yours, with real impact. A trivial one sounds evasive.","Say 'I made a mistake' early and clearly. Then spend most of the time on recovery and prevention.","End with the system-level guard, not just 'I will be more careful'."],followUps:["What is the difference between static and dynamic partition overwrite?","What if S3 versioning had not been enabled?","How did the analysts and your lead react?"],redFlags:["A 'mistake' that was really someone else's fault.","Hiding the mistake or fixing it quietly without telling consumers.","The only lesson is 'be more careful'."]},{id:"fail-missed-deadline",q:"Tell me about a time you missed a deadline or a commitment.",category:"failure",principles:["Earn Trust","Deliver Results","Are Right, A Lot"],listeningFor:"Honest ownership of the miss, how early you raised the risk, the options you offered, and a concrete change in how you estimate or communicate now.",example:{situation:"During a migration from Informatica to Spark on EMR, I estimated I could migrate the 20 inventory-domain jobs in six weeks. The business planned to drop the Informatica licence at the end of the quarter.",task:"I owned both the estimate and the delivery for those 20 jobs.",action:"By week four I had finished only 9. Several jobs had undocumented lookups and stored procedures I had missed because I estimated from job names and sizes. My real mistake was trying to catch up quietly for another week before raising it. When I did, I brought options: four more weeks, or keeping Informatica for the 5 hardest jobs for one extra month at a known licence cost. My manager chose the second. I then spent two days profiling every remaining job end to end, re-estimated with a buffer, and reported progress against the new plan every week.",result:"15 jobs went live on the original date and the last 5 three weeks later, at the cost of one extra month of licence. Since then I spike the riskiest items before estimating, and I raise a risk as soon as I am 20% behind, not when I am sure I will miss. On my next migration the estimate was within a week."},tips:["Admit where your own judgement failed (the estimate, the late escalation).","Show that you came with options, not just bad news.","Give a measurable change in your behaviour afterwards."],followUps:["Why did you wait a week before raising it?","How do you estimate migrations now?","How did your manager react?"],redFlags:["Blaming unclear requirements or other teams entirely.","Claiming you have never missed a deadline."]},{id:"fail-wrong-numbers",q:"Tell me about a time data you delivered turned out to be wrong.",category:"failure",principles:["Earn Trust","Dive Deep","Insist on the Highest Standards"],listeningFor:"How you handle a data-quality failure that reached stakeholders: speed of acknowledgement, rigour of the root-cause analysis, clear communication of what changed, and a check that prevents it next time.",example:{situation:"I built the daily revenue table behind the leadership dashboard at a D2C brand with about 30,000 orders a day. Three weeks after launch, finance noticed our daily numbers differed from their payment-gateway reports by 1-3%.",task:"It was my pipeline and my bug to find, and leadership had already used the numbers in a weekly review.",action:"I acknowledged it to the finance lead within the hour and promised a cause by end of day. Comparing one day order by order, every mismatched order was placed between midnight and 5:30 AM IST. The source stored timestamps in UTC and I had taken the date before converting to IST, so those orders were counted in the previous day. I fixed the conversion, backfilled three weeks, and added a check that compares daily totals with the gateway settlement file and fails the job above a 0.5% difference. I sent leadership a short note listing which numbers had changed and by how much.",result:"After the backfill, the difference dropped to about 0.05%, explained by refund timing. The reconciliation check has since caught two other upstream problems before anyone saw them. I now define timezone handling explicitly in every pipeline spec and test boundaries with orders at 23:59 and 00:01 IST."},tips:["Show the debugging method: narrow down by comparing at row level.","Explain exactly how you told stakeholders what had changed.","End with an automated check, not a promise."],followUps:["How would you have found this before launch?","How did you backfill without disrupting the dashboard?","What else in your pipelines could have the same timezone issue?"],redFlags:["Waiting until you have the full fix before acknowledging the problem.","Quietly correcting historical numbers without telling anyone."]},{id:"fail-tech-bet",q:"Tell me about a technical decision or bet of yours that did not work out.",category:"failure",principles:["Are Right, A Lot","Learn and Be Curious","Earn Trust"],listeningFor:"Whether you can recognise when your own idea is failing, reverse it with data, and learn something transferable. Defending a failing design out of pride is the red flag.",example:{situation:"At a logistics startup, I proposed moving our shipment-tracking table from hourly batch to Apache Hudi merge-on-read with near real-time upserts, because ops wanted fresher data. The table had about 2 billion rows and took up to 40,000 updates a minute.",task:"I led the design and implementation over two months.",action:"It worked in testing, but in production the updates touched many old partitions, so compaction ran constantly and soon cost more than the original batch. Analyst queries got slower because readers had to merge log files. I tuned file sizes and compaction schedules for three weeks without enough improvement. Then I went back to ops and asked what freshness each use case really needed: only one dashboard needed data within 15 minutes. I recommended rolling the big table back to batch and building a small streaming table for active shipments only, and I presented that to my manager as a reversal of my own proposal.",result:"The hybrid design cost about 35% less than the Hudi setup and met the 15-minute need. I lost around six weeks. I learned to confirm the real freshness requirement per consumer, and to load-test with production-like update patterns rather than uniform test data, before committing to a design."},tips:["Say early that it was your idea. It makes the reversal more credible.","Show the data that told you it was failing.","The lesson should be about how you make decisions, not just about the tool."],followUps:["Would copy-on-write tables have helped?","How did you decide when to stop tuning and roll back?","How did the ops team react to the change of plan?"],redFlags:["Blaming the tool ('Hudi is bad') rather than the fit.","No numbers on the cost of the failure."]},{id:"conf-manager",q:"Tell me about a time you disagreed with your manager.",category:"conflict",principles:["Have Backbone; Disagree and Commit","Insist on the Highest Standards","Customer Obsession"],listeningFor:"That you disagree with data and respect, understand your manager's constraints, offer an alternative, and then commit to the outcome. Both silent compliance and stubbornness lose points.",example:{situation:"We were launching a new seller-payout report with a date tied to a partner announcement. Two weeks out, my manager suggested skipping the reconciliation against the bank settlement file that I had planned, to save a week.",task:"I believed shipping payout numbers without reconciliation risked sellers' money and our credibility, but I also understood the date mattered.",action:"I asked for 20 minutes and came with data, not opinion: in the previous quarter, 3 of our 5 payout incidents would have been caught by exactly this check, and one had caused about Rs 4 lakh of incorrect payouts that took weeks to recover. I proposed a smaller version, reconciling totals per day and payment method, which I could build in 3 days, with row-level matching after launch. I also offered to take on-call for the report myself for the first two weeks.",result:"My manager agreed to the lighter version and we launched on time. In week one the check caught a missing settlement file that would have underpaid about 1,200 sellers. Row-level reconciliation followed three weeks later, and my manager asked me to present the approach to other teams. I learned that disagreement lands much better with a cheaper alternative in hand."},tips:["Show you understood why your manager wanted what they wanted.","Use evidence (past incidents, numbers) rather than principles alone.","Offer a middle path, and say what you would have done if overruled."],followUps:["What would you have done if your manager had still said no?","How did you raise it: in a meeting, in private, in writing?","Did it change your relationship?"],redFlags:["Escalating over your manager's head as the first move.","A story where you simply refused.","A story where you disagreed but said nothing."]},{id:"conf-senior-design",q:"Tell me about a time you disagreed with a technical decision and it went the other way.",category:"conflict",principles:["Have Backbone; Disagree and Commit","Are Right, A Lot","Deliver Results"],listeningFor:"The 'commit' half of disagree and commit: you made your case properly, accepted the decision, and then worked to make it succeed rather than proving yourself right.",example:{situation:"My team was choosing how to serve product analytics. Our principal engineer wanted to load everything into Redshift; I argued for an Iceberg lakehouse queried with Athena and Redshift Spectrum, because I expected fast data growth and wanted open formats.",task:"I would build most of it, so I needed to either change the decision or commit to it fully.",action:"I wrote a two-page comparison with cost estimates, query benchmarks on a 500 GB sample, and the team's skills. The benchmarks showed Redshift was 3-5x faster on the dashboard queries. The principal pointed out that we had about six months before volume would matter and that the analysts already knew Redshift. I still thought storage growth would hurt us and said so in the review, but the decision went to Redshift. I committed: I built the Redshift model, and made two choices that kept options open, landing raw and silver data as Parquet on S3 first and keeping transformations in portable SQL.",result:"We delivered in 7 weeks, about two weeks faster than my design would have taken, with dashboards loading in under 3 seconds. Eighteen months later, when storage did grow, the S3 layer made it easy to move older data to Spectrum. I learned the principal was right about speed to market, and that I can disagree and still make the chosen path succeed."},tips:["Make your original argument sound reasonable, then show you accepted the decision gracefully.","Show what you did to make the decision succeed, not just tolerate it.","It is fine if you were partly right later; do not gloat."],followUps:["Looking back, who was right?","When is it right to keep pushing after a decision is made?","How did you present the benchmarks?"],redFlags:["Complaining about the decision or the person.","Quietly building it your way anyway."]},{id:"conf-teammate",q:"Tell me about a conflict with a teammate and how you resolved it.",category:"conflict",principles:["Earn Trust","Insist on the Highest Standards","Strive to be Earth's Best Employer"],listeningFor:"Maturity: addressing it directly and privately, understanding the other person's constraints, and fixing the underlying problem rather than winning the argument.",example:{situation:"A technically strong teammate often merged Airflow DAG changes straight to main without tests or review. Twice in one month a broken import took down every DAG in our MWAA environment for about an hour.",task:"I was not his lead, but I was on call for both incidents and the pattern was hurting the whole team.",action:"Instead of raising it in the team channel, I asked him for a coffee and described the two incidents and their impact without blaming him. He explained that reviews often took two days and he was under pressure to ship. That was fair, so I suggested we fix both problems. I set up a CI check that loads every DAG into a DagBag and fails on import errors, plus unit tests for our shared operators, all running in about 3 minutes. I proposed to the team that small DAG changes get one reviewer within 4 hours, and volunteered as backup reviewer for the first month.",result:"We had zero DAG import outages in the next six months, and average review time fell from about 2 days to 5 hours. He became one of the most active reviewers on the team. I learned that the person causing a problem often has a real constraint, and removing that constraint works better than enforcing rules."},tips:["Talk privately first. Interviewers note whether you went public or to the manager immediately.","Describe the other person fairly; they are not the villain of the story.","Fix the system that caused the friction."],followUps:["What would you have done if he had refused to change?","How did the rest of the team react to the review SLA?","Have you ever been on the other side of this?"],redFlags:["Describing the teammate as lazy, incompetent or difficult.","Going straight to the manager without talking to them."]},{id:"conf-cross-team",q:"Tell me about a time two stakeholders wanted conflicting things from your data.",category:"conflict",principles:["Earn Trust","Customer Obsession","Dive Deep"],listeningFor:"That you can stay neutral, understand both sides' goals, use data to make the disagreement concrete, and drive a decision without taking it away from the business owners.",example:{situation:"At a subscription SaaS company, marketing and finance both used my revenue tables. Marketing's MRR counted trials converting at month end and finance's did not, so their numbers differed by about 6%, and the CEO asked why during board preparation.",task:"I owned the revenue model, so I took on getting one agreed definition, even though the decision belonged to them.",action:"I met each team separately to understand their reasoning: marketing cared about conversion momentum, finance about recognised revenue. I built a breakdown of the 6% gap into trials, mid-month upgrades and refunds. Then I brought both leads together with that breakdown and proposed two clearly named metrics from one model: recognised MRR as the official number and booked MRR for marketing's funnel, with the difference documented. I wrote both definitions into our dbt docs and metrics layer, and deprecated the old ad hoc columns with 30 days' notice.",result:"Both leads signed off within a week, and the board deck used recognised MRR with booked MRR as a secondary line. Questions about mismatched revenue in our data channel went from several a week to almost none. I learned that most data conflicts are really definition conflicts, and a clear breakdown takes the emotion out."},tips:["Stay neutral: your job is to make the trade-off visible, not to pick a winner.","Quantify the disagreement before the meeting.","Close with a durable artefact: documented definitions, a metrics layer, a deprecation plan."],followUps:["What if they had not agreed?","How did you handle dashboards built on the old columns?","Who owns metric definitions in your company now?"],redFlags:["Deciding the business definition yourself.","Building two separate pipelines to keep both sides happy."]},{id:"imp-cost",q:"Tell me about a time you significantly reduced cost.",category:"impact",principles:["Frugality","Dive Deep","Deliver Results"],listeningFor:"A methodical approach (measure first, attack the biggest items), specific techniques you understand, the trade-offs you accepted, and a credible before-and-after number.",example:{situation:"Our EMR bill for the analytics platform had grown to about Rs 9 lakh a month, and the CFO asked every team to cut cloud spend by 20% without slowing delivery.",task:"I volunteered to lead the data platform's part, which was the largest single line item.",action:"I started by tagging every cluster and job to see cost per pipeline, which showed that 6 of 70 jobs made up 55% of spend. In the Spark UI, most of their executors were 40-50% idle because the jobs were sized for sale-day peaks. I moved task nodes to spot using instance fleets across 4 instance types, kept core nodes on-demand, switched to Graviton instances after a benchmark showed equal runtimes, and enabled managed scaling. I compacted about 2 million small files in the clickstream table, which cut both runtime and S3 request costs. I also found 40 TB of intermediate data nobody read and added S3 lifecycle rules to expire it.",result:"Monthly spend fell from about Rs 9 lakh to about Rs 5.5 lakh, a 39% cut, with no SLA misses over the next quarter. Spot interruptions caused 2 retries in three months, which we accepted. The cost-per-pipeline dashboard I built is now reviewed by the team every month."},tips:["Show that you measured before cutting: cost per pipeline, utilisation.","Name the trade-offs (spot interruptions, scaling lag) and how you handled them.","Give a monthly or yearly figure and a percentage."],followUps:["Why keep core nodes on-demand?","How did you measure that Graviton gave equal runtimes?","How did the small files get there in the first place?"],redFlags:["Cuts that quietly lowered reliability or freshness.","Being unable to break the saving down into its parts."]},{id:"imp-performance",q:"Tell me about the biggest performance improvement you have delivered.",category:"impact",principles:["Dive Deep","Customer Obsession","Deliver Results"],listeningFor:"Diagnosis with the engine's own tools, fixes grounded in how the engine works, and a result that mattered to users, not just a faster query.",example:{situation:"Our sales dashboard on Redshift served about 300 users in category and supply teams, and its main page took 35-45 seconds to load. At the morning peak, queries queued or timed out, and users had started exporting to Excel instead.",task:"I was asked to get the main page under 5 seconds without moving to a bigger cluster.",action:"I pulled the slowest queries from the system tables and found three problems. The 1.8 billion-row sales fact was distributed EVEN, so every join to the product dimension redistributed data across nodes. Most queries filtered on date, but the sort key was order_id. And the dashboard ran the same 12 heavy aggregations for every user. I redistributed the fact on product_id, the most common join key and evenly spread, and sorted it on sale_date. I built three materialized views for the dashboard aggregations, refreshed after each load, and gave dashboard queries their own WLM queue so ad hoc analyst queries could not block them. I tested each change on a snapshot cluster first.",result:"The main page now loads in 2-3 seconds, peak queue time dropped from minutes to near zero, and timeouts stopped. Weekly active users grew from 300 to over 450 in two months, which mattered more to the business than the speed itself."},tips:["Name the diagnostic tools you used and what they showed.","Explain each fix in terms of how the engine works (data movement, pruning, precomputation).","Connect the speed-up to a user or business outcome."],followUps:["Why product_id and not customer_id as the distribution key?","What did the materialized views cost in refresh time and storage?","How would you approach the same problem on Snowflake or BigQuery?"],redFlags:["'I added indexes' (Redshift does not have conventional indexes).","No baseline measurement."]},{id:"imp-biggest",q:"What is the most impactful thing you have done in your current role?",category:"impact",principles:["Deliver Results","Customer Obsession","Think Big"],listeningFor:"Business impact, not technical elegance. They want to hear a result the business measured, your specific contribution, and how you de-risked it.",example:{situation:"At a lending fintech, the risk team scored new loan applications using customer features refreshed once a day. Fraud rings had learned to apply many times within a few hours, before the daily refresh caught the pattern.",task:"I proposed and owned moving the six most important velocity features, such as applications per device and per bank account in the last 24 hours, to near real time.",action:"I worked with risk analysts to pick the features that would actually change decisions instead of moving all 120. I built a Spark Structured Streaming job that read application events from Kafka, computed sliding-window counts by device and account, and wrote them to DynamoDB for the scoring service to read in single-digit milliseconds. The daily batch features stayed the source of truth, and a nightly job compared streaming and batch values for the same keys and alerted on more than 1% disagreement. I ran it in shadow mode for three weeks so risk could compare decisions before switching.",result:"Feature freshness went from up to 24 hours to under 2 minutes. In the first quarter the risk team blocked about 900 more fraudulent applications and estimated roughly Rs 40 lakh in avoided losses, for about Rs 60,000 a month in running cost. I am proudest of it because the business measured the impact, not me."},tips:["Pick the story with the biggest business result, even if the tech was simpler than another story.","Say who measured the impact and how.","Show how you reduced risk: shadow mode, parallel runs, reconciliation."],followUps:["How do you keep streaming and batch features consistent?","What happens to the scoring service if the streaming job is down?","Why DynamoDB and not Redis?"],redFlags:["Impact described only in technical terms (latency, lines of code).","Claiming the whole team's or the business's result as your own."]},{id:"imp-tight-deadline",q:"Tell me about a time you delivered under a tight deadline.",category:"impact",principles:["Deliver Results","Bias for Action","Customer Obsession"],listeningFor:"Prioritisation under pressure: finding the real risks quickly, focusing on what matters most, having a fallback plan, and not cutting corners that would hurt later.",example:{situation:"Three weeks before our biggest annual festival sale, the business told us to expect 6x normal order volume for five days. In the previous year's sale, our hourly pipelines had fallen 4 hours behind on day one, leaving category managers blind.",task:"I was made responsible for the 8 hourly pipelines behind the sale war-room dashboards holding up at 6x.",action:"First I load-tested by replaying last year's sale-day Kafka data at 6x into staging. That exposed two bottlenecks: a join that shuffled the whole product catalogue every hour, and Redshift COPY loading thousands of small files. I changed the join to broadcast a filtered catalogue and made the Spark job write fewer files of about 256 MB. I pre-scaled the EMR cluster on a schedule for the sale window instead of trusting autoscaling to react in time, and agreed a degraded mode with the dashboard owners: if the pipelines fell behind, I would publish the top 50 categories first. I wrote a runbook and shared on-call with one teammate for the five days.",result:"At peak we processed about 7x normal volume, every hourly run finished within 22 minutes, and dashboards were never more than an hour stale. We never needed the degraded mode. The load-test setup is now reused before every sale event."},tips:["Show how you found the real risks fast (a load test beats guessing).","Mention the fallback plan you agreed with stakeholders.","Say what you chose not to do, given the time."],followUps:["Why not rely on EMR managed scaling?","How did you size the cluster for the sale?","What would the degraded mode have looked like in practice?"],redFlags:["Heroics with no plan: 'I worked 18-hour days'.","Skipping testing to hit the date."]},{id:"amb-unclear-requirements",q:"Tell me about a time you had to deliver with unclear requirements.",category:"ambiguity",principles:["Customer Obsession","Bias for Action","Invent and Simplify"],listeningFor:"How you turn a vague request into a concrete, agreed scope: talking to users, finding the decisions behind the request, shipping a small version and iterating.",example:{situation:"A business head asked my team for 'a customer 360 table' for marketing, support and product. There was no spec and no named owner, and each team imagined something different.",task:"I was asked to lead it, and my first job was to turn a vague request into something we could ship and measure.",action:"I spent 30 minutes with one or two people from each team and asked what decision they would make with the table that they could not make today. That gave me 14 concrete questions, such as which customers ordered in the last 30 days but also opened a support ticket. Grouping them showed that 10 of the 14 needed only about 25 attributes from three sources we already had. I wrote a one-page scope with what v1 would and would not include, its grain (one row per customer per day), freshness (daily by 8 AM) and owners, and got the business head to sign it. I shipped v1 in three weeks and booked a review with each team after two weeks of use.",result:"Ten of the 14 questions were answerable at launch, and the table had 60 weekly users within a month. The v2 backlog came from real usage, and two attributes we had planned turned out to be unneeded, so we never built them. I now ask 'what decision will this drive?' before designing any dataset."},tips:["Show the questions you asked to remove ambiguity.","Write down and agree a scope, including what is out.","Ship something small fast, then iterate on real feedback."],followUps:["How did you handle the 4 questions you did not cover?","What would you do if two teams wanted conflicting definitions of 'active customer'?","How did you choose the grain?"],redFlags:["Waiting for someone else to write the requirements.","Building everything everyone asked for in one big release."]},{id:"amb-incomplete-data",q:"Tell me about a decision you had to make quickly without all the information.",category:"ambiguity",principles:["Bias for Action","Are Right, A Lot","Earn Trust"],listeningFor:"Judgement under uncertainty: what you checked quickly, how you weighed the cost of each possible error, whether the decision was reversible, and how you communicated it.",example:{situation:"One morning, about an hour before the 9 AM finance refresh, our checks showed the orders table had 4% more rows than usual. The upstream team was not online yet, and I could not tell whether it was a real spike or duplicated events.",task:"I had to decide whether to publish on time or hold the data, without knowing the cause.",action:"I checked what I could in 15 minutes: the extra rows had the same order_ids with different ingestion timestamps, and revenue per order looked normal, which strongly suggested a replay. I weighed the two possible errors: publishing duplicates would overstate revenue in a report leadership used, while an hour or two of delay was inconvenient but fully reversible. I held the publish and told finance at 8:30 that data would be late and why. I deduplicated on order_id, keeping the latest version, in a separate table, compared it with the previous seven days and with payment counts, which matched within 0.1%, and published at 10:15.",result:"Finance got correct numbers about 75 minutes late. The upstream team later confirmed a consumer had replayed 6 hours of data after a deploy. I made deduplication on order_id a permanent pipeline step, so a similar replay a month later caused no delay at all. I learned to weigh decisions by how reversible the possible mistake is."},tips:["Explain the quick checks you did and what they told you.","Name the trade-off explicitly: the cost of being wrong each way.","Show you communicated the decision before acting on it."],followUps:["What if the 4% had turned out to be real orders?","Who would normally make that call?","How does the dedup step choose which version to keep?"],redFlags:["Waiting for full certainty and missing the window anyway.","Making a risky irreversible call without telling anyone."]},{id:"amb-no-documentation",q:"Tell me about a time you had to work on something with no documentation or anyone to ask.",category:"ambiguity",principles:["Dive Deep","Learn and Be Curious","Insist on the Highest Standards"],listeningFor:"A systematic way to reverse-engineer an unknown system, validating your understanding with the business, and a safe migration with parallel runs.",example:{situation:"I inherited a 3,000-line SQL stored procedure that calculated weekly commission payouts for about 8,000 delivery partners. The author had left, there were no docs, and the business wanted it moved off the old Oracle database within two months.",task:"I had to understand exactly what it did, including undocumented rules, and migrate it to Spark without changing anyone's payout.",action:"Instead of reading it top to bottom, I mapped it into 11 logical steps by its intermediate tables and documented each step, with a list of questions for every oddity. I found 7 hard-coded rules, such as a bonus that applied only in two cities, and took them to the operations team: 2 were obsolete and 5 were still valid. I rewrote each step as a tested PySpark function, then ran the old and new pipelines in parallel for four weekly cycles and compared payouts per partner to the rupee. Every difference had to be explained before go-live.",result:"The first parallel run had 312 mismatches and the fourth had none. We switched over on schedule, and with ops approval I removed the 2 obsolete rules. The documented rule list is now the reference ops use when they change commission policy."},tips:["Describe your method for breaking down the unknown system.","Validate the business rules with people, not just the code.","Prove equivalence with parallel runs and explain the mismatches."],followUps:["What kinds of mismatches did the first parallel run show?","How did you test the PySpark functions?","What would you have done if ops could not explain a rule?"],redFlags:["Rewriting from scratch based on what you think it should do.","Going live without a parallel run on financial data."]},{id:"cust-say-no",q:"Tell me about a time you said no to a stakeholder.",category:"customer",principles:["Customer Obsession","Frugality","Have Backbone; Disagree and Commit"],listeningFor:"That you say no to the solution, not to the customer: understand the real need, explain costs honestly, and offer something that meets the need better.",example:{situation:"A product manager asked me to make our app engagement dashboard real time for a new feature launch. The data came from a daily batch, and real time would need a streaming pipeline and a new serving store.",task:"I estimated about 6 weeks of work and roughly Rs 1.5 lakh a month to run, so before saying yes I needed to understand what 'real time' meant to them.",action:"I asked the PM to walk me through what they would do with the numbers. The real need was to catch a broken rollout within the first hour of a release and roll it back. That needed a few metrics, crash rate and feature adoption, refreshed every 15 minutes, not every second and not the whole dashboard. I said no to rebuilding the dashboard in real time, explained the cost and timeline honestly, and proposed a 15-minute micro-batch pipeline for just those metrics, with an alert on crash-rate spikes. I agreed a date with the PM and kept the daily dashboard as it was.",result:"I shipped it in 8 days for under Rs 20,000 a month. During launch week the crash-rate alert caught a bad Android build within 40 minutes, and the team rolled back before most users saw it. The PM now brings me into requirement discussions earlier, which is the best outcome of all."},tips:["Ask 'what will you do with it?' before estimating anything.","Put numbers on the cost of the original request.","Offer an alternative that meets the real need."],followUps:["What if the PM had insisted on full real time?","How did you estimate the cost?","How do you decide what freshness a dataset really needs?"],redFlags:["Saying no because it was hard or boring.","Saying yes to everything and missing deadlines."]},{id:"cust-real-need",q:"Tell me about a time you went beyond what a stakeholder asked for to solve their real problem.",category:"customer",principles:["Customer Obsession","Invent and Simplify","Ownership"],listeningFor:"Curiosity about the decision behind a request, and building something that removes recurring manual work rather than just completing tickets.",example:{situation:"Every Monday, an analyst on the category team asked me for a CSV of the past week's sales by SKU, store and promotion. It took me about an hour each week, and other analysts started asking for slightly different versions.",task:"I could keep running the queries, but I wanted to understand why they needed it and stop being the bottleneck.",action:"I sat with the analyst for half an hour to watch what they did with the file. They pasted it into a spreadsheet, joined it to a promotion calendar they maintained by hand, and calculated promotion uplift. So the real need was uplift, not the CSV. I built a gold table at SKU-store-day grain with promotion flags, moved the promotion calendar into a managed table the category team could edit through a simple form, and pre-computed baseline and uplift. I exposed it in Athena and a dashboard, ran a 45-minute session for 12 analysts, and answered their questions for two weeks.",result:"The weekly requests stopped, saving me about 4 hours a week across all the variants, and analysts could see uplift daily instead of weekly. In the next quarter the category team used it to cut two promotions that were losing money. I learned to ask about the decision behind any recurring request."},tips:["Show the moment you discovered the real need.","Quantify the time saved and the business decision it enabled.","Mention how you drove adoption, not just that you built it."],followUps:["How did you calculate the baseline for uplift?","What did you do about the promotion calendar's data quality?","How do you decide when a recurring request deserves a product?"],redFlags:["Building something bigger than needed without checking with the user.","No evidence anyone used it."]},{id:"cust-bad-news",q:"Tell me about a time you had to deliver bad news to a stakeholder.",category:"customer",principles:["Earn Trust","Customer Obsession","Ownership"],listeningFor:"Early, honest, specific communication with a plan and a workaround. They want to see you protect the stakeholder's trust, not your image.",example:{situation:"Two days before quarter-end, an outage in the upstream billing system meant about 18 hours of invoice data had not reached our lake. The finance controller's team needed complete data for the quarterly close, and billing said recovery would take at least a day.",task:"I owned the finance data pipeline, so it was my job to tell finance and give them something they could plan around.",action:"I did not wait for all the answers. Within an hour I messaged the finance controller with what I knew: which data was missing, which reports were affected and which were safe, and that I would update every four hours. I worked with the billing team on recovery and prepared a fallback: an estimate of the missing invoices from the payment-gateway feed, clearly labelled provisional, so finance could prepare drafts. When the billing data arrived the next evening, I backfilled, reconciled totals against the gateway, and sent a final note listing what had changed from the provisional numbers.",result:"Finance closed the quarter on time with complete data, and the provisional numbers were within 0.7% of the final ones. The controller told my manager the regular updates made it the least stressful delay they had experienced. I learned that early, honest updates with a workaround build more trust than a perfect fix delivered late."},tips:["Show how fast you communicated and what you said.","Separate what is affected from what is safe.","Offer a workaround and a time for the next update."],followUps:["How did you make sure nobody used the provisional numbers as final?","What would you have done if billing could not recover the data?","How do you decide the update frequency during an incident?"],redFlags:["Waiting until you have a fix before telling anyone.","Blaming the upstream team in front of the stakeholder."]},{id:"grow-learn-fast",q:"Tell me about a time you had to learn a new technology quickly.",category:"growth",principles:["Learn and Be Curious","Deliver Results","Bias for Action"],listeningFor:"How you learn: fundamentals first, hands-on experiments, using other people's experience, and getting to production safely. Plus a clear delivery result.",example:{situation:"My team decided to replace nightly database dumps with CDC for 40 Postgres tables, using Debezium and Kafka on Amazon MSK. I had used Kafka only as a consumer and had never run Kafka Connect or Debezium, and we had six weeks to get the first 10 tables live.",task:"I was the engineer assigned to build and own the CDC pipeline.",action:"I spent the first three days on fundamentals: the Debezium docs, Postgres logical replication, and how Connect stores offsets. I set up a local stack with Docker Compose and deliberately broke it, killing a connector mid-snapshot, dropping a column, restarting with a stale replication slot, so I knew what failures looked like before production showed me. I found an engineer in another team who had run Debezium and booked an hour with him, which saved me from the replication-slot disk growth problem he had hit. I wrote a runbook as I went, and got the first two tables live in week three so I learned from production early.",result:"All 10 tables went live in five weeks, a week early, and the other 30 followed within two months. Dumps that took 3 hours were replaced by data less than 5 minutes old. My runbook became the team's CDC onboarding doc, and I now use the same break-it-locally-first approach with every new tool."},tips:["Describe your learning method, not just the fact that you learned.","Mention who you learned from. It shows you use the organisation well.","Show a delivery result, not only a certificate."],followUps:["What is the replication-slot disk growth problem and how do you prevent it?","How does Debezium handle the initial snapshot?","What would you learn differently next time?"],redFlags:["'I did an online course' with no application.","Learning on production with no safe experiments first."]},{id:"grow-feedback",q:"Tell me about a time you received critical feedback. What did you do with it?",category:"growth",principles:["Learn and Be Curious","Earn Trust","Strive to be Earth's Best Employer"],listeningFor:"Whether you can take feedback without defensiveness, turn it into specific changed behaviour, and show evidence that the change stuck.",example:{situation:"After a pipeline incident that took 6 hours to resolve, my manager told me in our one-to-one that my technical fix had been good, but stakeholders had heard nothing from me for the first four hours and had escalated to her.",task:"It was hard to hear after working through the night, but it was true: I tended to go heads-down and communicate only once I had an answer.",action:"I asked her what good looked like, and she pointed me to a senior engineer on another team who ran incidents well. I shadowed him on two incidents and saw that he posted a short update every 30-60 minutes even when there was no news. I wrote a simple template for myself: what is affected, what we know, what we are doing, when the next update is. I proposed adding it to our on-call runbook with a rule that on-call posts a first update within 15 minutes of a page. For my next few incidents, I asked a teammate to tell me if I went quiet.",result:"On my next major incident, a late partner file that delayed a dashboard, I posted the first update in 10 minutes and then every 45 minutes, and the stakeholder thanked us in the channel for the clarity. The whole team now uses the template. My manager mentioned the change in my next review, and I learned to ask for specific examples whenever I get feedback."},tips:["Pick real feedback about a real weakness, not a disguised strength.","Show the specific behaviour you changed, and evidence it stuck.","Show you sought out a role model or more feedback."],followUps:["How did you feel when you heard it?","What feedback have you received recently?","Have you given difficult feedback to someone else?"],redFlags:["'My weakness is that I work too hard.'","Getting defensive or explaining why the feedback was unfair."]},{id:"grow-mentor",q:"Tell me about a time you helped a teammate grow.",category:"growth",principles:["Hire and Develop the Best","Strive to be Earth's Best Employer","Ownership"],listeningFor:"Deliberate mentoring with a goal and a plan, not just answering questions. Mid-level candidates are expected to multiply the team, even without a lead title.",example:{situation:"A fresher joined our team of six and was due to join the on-call rotation in two months. Our pipelines covered 60 DAGs across three AWS accounts, and the previous new joiner had struggled badly in their first on-call week.",task:"My manager asked me to be his onboarding buddy. I set the goal that he could handle a typical on-call week without me by the end of the two months.",action:"In week one I gave him a real but low-risk task, fixing a flaky data-quality check, so he touched the whole flow from code to Airflow to S3. Each week I walked him through one past incident from our post-mortems, and I asked how he would debug it before showing what we had done. In week five he shadowed my on-call, and in week seven I shadowed his, stepping in only when asked. In code reviews I explained the reason behind each comment and marked comments as must-fix or optional so he was not overwhelmed. I also asked him to fix our onboarding doc wherever it was wrong or confusing.",result:"He took his first solo on-call in week nine and resolved 4 of 5 alerts on his own. His doc fixes updated about 20 stale sections, and the next new joiner reached solo on-call in seven weeks. Mentoring also made me better at explaining our design choices, which helped me in design reviews."},tips:["Show a goal and a plan, not just 'I helped him whenever he asked'.","Give evidence of their growth, measured by what they could do alone.","Mention what you learned from mentoring."],followUps:["What did you do when he made a mistake?","How did you adapt your approach to how he learned?","How do you balance mentoring with your own delivery?"],redFlags:["Taking credit for the other person's achievements.","Mentoring that was really just doing their work for them."]}],i=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:a},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),o=[{name:"Customer Obsession",meaning:"Start from what the customer actually needs and work backwards, and keep their trust even when it costs you short-term convenience.",inDataEngineering:"Your customers are analysts, data scientists, finance, product teams and the end users of data products. It means asking what decision a table drives before building it, treating a late dashboard as a customer problem rather than a ticket, and measuring freshness and correctness from the consumer's point of view.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you went out of your way to understand what a data consumer really needed.","Describe a time a stakeholder asked for one thing but needed something else. What did you do?","Tell me about a time you had to balance a customer's request against data quality or platform health."]},{name:"Ownership",meaning:"Act like an owner of the whole outcome, think long term, and never say 'that is not my job' when something your work touches is broken.",inDataEngineering:"Fixing the upstream schema issue instead of just patching your job, writing the runbook nobody asked for, staying with an incident until the downstream report is correct, and cleaning up the cost and tech debt your pipelines create.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you took on something outside your area of responsibility.","Describe a time you saw a problem in a pipeline you did not own. What did you do?","Tell me about a long-term investment you made that slowed you down in the short term."]},{name:"Invent and Simplify",meaning:"Look for new and simpler ways of doing things, borrow good ideas from anywhere, and accept being misunderstood for a while when you try something new.",inDataEngineering:"Replacing 40 copy-pasted Glue jobs with one config-driven framework, deleting a fragile stage nobody needs, or turning a manual monthly backfill into a one-command tool.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you simplified a complex process or pipeline.","Describe something you built that others on your team now reuse."]},{name:"Are Right, A Lot",meaning:"Have good judgement and instincts, seek out views that contradict yours, and change your mind when the evidence says so.",inDataEngineering:"Choosing the right file format, partitioning or engine based on evidence (benchmarks, query patterns, cost), and admitting when a design you championed was the wrong call.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you made a technical decision with incomplete information. How did it turn out?","Describe a time you changed your mind after hearing a different point of view.","Tell me about a time your judgement was wrong."]},{name:"Learn and Be Curious",meaning:"Keep learning, stay curious about how things work, and explore new possibilities.",inDataEngineering:"Reading Spark execution plans instead of guessing, picking up Kafka or Iceberg because a problem needs it, and asking why a metric moved rather than just shipping the number.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a technology you learned quickly to deliver a project.","What is something you learned recently that changed how you work?"]},{name:"Hire and Develop the Best",meaning:"Raise the bar with every hire, recognise talent, and take coaching and developing others seriously.",inDataEngineering:"Even without a lead title: mentoring a new joiner through their first on-call, running interview loops, writing onboarding docs, and reviewing code in a way that teaches rather than just blocks.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you helped a teammate grow.","Describe a time you gave someone difficult feedback."]},{name:"Insist on the Highest Standards",meaning:"Hold a bar that others may think is unreasonably high, keep raising it, and do not let defects travel downstream.",inDataEngineering:"Adding data-quality checks, tests and reconciliation before a pipeline goes live, refusing to ship numbers you cannot reconcile, and fixing root causes instead of rerunning jobs.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you refused to compromise on quality.","Describe a time you raised the quality bar for your team.","Tell me about a time you were not satisfied with the status quo."]},{name:"Think Big",meaning:"Set a bold direction that inspires results, and look beyond the immediate task for bigger opportunities to serve customers.",inDataEngineering:"Designing the ingestion pattern so the next 100 tables are a config change, proposing a self-serve platform instead of one more bespoke pipeline, or planning for 10x data volume.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you proposed something much bigger than what you were asked to do.","Describe a design you made that anticipated future scale."]},{name:"Bias for Action",meaning:"Speed matters. Many decisions can be reversed, so take calculated risks and move rather than waiting for perfect information.",inDataEngineering:"Shipping a working daily batch now and iterating towards streaming later, pausing a broken job during an incident without waiting for a meeting, or prototyping a fix in a day to settle a debate.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you made a decision quickly without all the data.","Describe a time you took a calculated risk.","Tell me about a time you had to act before you had approval."]},{name:"Frugality",meaning:"Do more with less. Constraints breed resourcefulness, and headcount, budget or fixed spend are not the measure of success.",inDataEngineering:"Cutting EMR or Glue spend with right-sizing and spot, deleting unused tables and snapshots, using S3 lifecycle rules, and choosing the boring cheap tool when it meets the SLA.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you reduced cost without hurting quality.","Describe a time you delivered with fewer resources than you wanted."]},{name:"Earn Trust",meaning:"Listen carefully, speak honestly, treat people with respect, admit your own mistakes openly, and compare yourself against the best.",inDataEngineering:"Telling stakeholders early that a number was wrong and when it will be fixed, writing honest post-mortems that name your own mistake, and keeping promises on SLAs.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you had to deliver bad news to a stakeholder.","Describe a time you made a mistake that affected others. How did you handle it?"]},{name:"Dive Deep",meaning:"Stay connected to the details, check things yourself, be sceptical when metrics and anecdotes disagree, and no task is beneath you.",inDataEngineering:"Reading the Spark UI to find the skewed stage, tracing a 2% revenue mismatch to a timezone bug, and checking row counts yourself instead of trusting 'it succeeded'.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about the hardest bug you have debugged.","Describe a time a metric looked wrong. How did you find the cause?","Tell me about a time you found a problem others had missed."]},{name:"Have Backbone; Disagree and Commit",meaning:"Challenge decisions you disagree with respectfully and with evidence, even when it is uncomfortable, and once a decision is made, commit to it fully.",inDataEngineering:"Pushing back on a design that will not meet the SLA, or on a deadline that would ship unreconciled numbers, with data. Then backing the final call wholeheartedly even if it went the other way.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you strongly disagreed with your manager or a senior engineer.","Describe a time you committed to a decision you did not agree with.","Tell me about a time you pushed back on a stakeholder."]},{name:"Deliver Results",meaning:"Focus on the inputs that matter and deliver them with the right quality and on time, and do not give up when things go wrong.",inDataEngineering:"Landing the migration by the cut-over date, hitting the 7 AM SLA every day, and being able to quote the before-and-after numbers for runtime, cost and incidents.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you delivered a project under a tight deadline.","Describe a time you hit a major obstacle. How did you still deliver?","What is the most impactful thing you have delivered?"]},{name:"Strive to be Earth's Best Employer",meaning:"Work to make the workplace safer, fairer, more productive and more empathetic, and help the people around you grow and enjoy their work.",inDataEngineering:"Fixing a noisy on-call rotation that is burning people out, sharing knowledge so one person is not a single point of failure, and including quieter teammates in design discussions.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you improved how your team works day to day.","Describe a time you noticed a teammate struggling. What did you do?"]},{name:"Success and Scale Bring Broad Responsibility",meaning:"Size brings impact on customers, communities and the world, so think carefully about the second-order effects of what you build and leave things better than you found them.",inDataEngineering:"Protecting personal data (masking PII, honouring deletion requests under laws like the DPDP Act or GDPR), limiting access to sensitive tables, and cutting wasteful compute that has a real energy and cost footprint.",sampleQuestions:["Tell me about a time you considered the wider impact of a technical decision.","Describe a time you protected customer data or privacy, even when it slowed you down."]}],s=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:o},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),e=(...t)=>t.join(`
`),n={star:[{title:"What STAR is and why interviewers want it",body:e("STAR is a way to tell a work story so the interviewer can score it quickly: **Situation, Task, Action, Result**. Behavioural interviewers, especially at Amazon, write notes against a rubric. If they cannot find what YOU did and what changed because of it, they cannot give you credit, however good the work was.","","- **Situation**: the context. Company, team, system, what was at stake. Include one number (data volume, users, SLA, money).","- **Task**: what you specifically owned. The goal, the constraint, the deadline.","- **Action**: the steps you took, in order, and why you chose them. This is where the hiring decision is made.","- **Result**: the measurable outcome, plus what you learned or would do differently.","","Think of it as a 2-minute answer that invites follow-up questions, not a 10-minute monologue. A good STAR answer is 150-300 words spoken.")},{title:"The time split: keep Situation short, make Action long",body:e("The most common failure is spending a minute on context and twenty seconds on what you did. Aim for roughly:","","- **Situation**: 10-15% (2-3 sentences)","- **Task**: 10% (1-2 sentences)","- **Action**: 50-60% (3-5 concrete steps)","- **Result**: 20-25% (numbers, then the lesson)","","For a technical audience, the Action should contain real decisions: 'I salted the join key into 16 buckets because one merchant held 30% of rows', not 'I optimised the job'. Each step should answer 'why that and not something else?' before they ask.")},{title:"Quantify the result",body:e("Numbers make a story believable and make it easy for the interviewer to argue for you in the debrief. Data engineering gives you plenty:","","- **Runtime and freshness**: 'from 4 h 10 min to 55 min', 'data available by 6:30 AM instead of 11 AM'","- **Cost**: 'EMR spend down from about Rs 9 lakh to Rs 5.5 lakh a month', '38% fewer DPU-hours'","- **Reliability**: 'SLA misses down from 6 a month to 0 for the next quarter', 'on-call pages halved'","- **Quality**: 'duplicate orders in the fact table from 0.8% to under 0.01%'","- **Adoption and people**: '3 other teams now use the framework', '60 tables onboarded by config'","","If you do not have exact figures, give honest approximations ('roughly 40%', 'about 2 TB a day') and be ready to explain how you measured them. Never invent precision you cannot defend: interviewers probe numbers.")},{title:"Say 'I', not 'we'",body:e("Data engineering is team work, so candidates naturally say 'we built', 'we decided'. The interviewer is hiring you, not your team, and 'we' leaves them unable to tell whether you led the work or watched it.","","- Use 'I' for what you did: 'I wrote the dedup logic', 'I proposed', 'I convinced the analytics lead'.","- Use 'we' or 'the team' only for context, and say your part straight after: 'The team owned the migration; I owned the 40 finance tables and the reconciliation framework.'","- Give credit where it is due ('my teammate built the Kafka side'). That reads as honesty, not weakness.","","Practise by recording yourself and counting the 'we's. Interviewers at Amazon will literally ask 'what did YOU do?' if you slip.")},{title:"Build a story bank before the interview",body:e("You do not need 30 stories. You need **6-8 strong stories** that each flex across several questions. One good incident story can answer 'a failure', 'working under pressure', 'a time you dived deep' and 'earning trust'.","","- Your main project (architecture, the hardest problem, what you would change)","- An incident or failure you caused or owned, with the fix and the prevention","- A disagreement with a manager, senior engineer or stakeholder","- A cost or performance win with numbers","- A time you worked with unclear requirements","- A time you said no to or negotiated with a stakeholder","- A time you learned something fast, and a time you mentored or gave feedback","","For each, note the numbers, the 3-4 key actions and the lesson. Use the 'My stories' tab to write them and check that every category is covered. For Amazon, map each story to 2-3 Leadership Principles and make sure the 16 are covered between them; most loops probe 6-10 principles.","","Use recent stories (last 2-3 years) where possible, and never reuse the same story twice in one loop: interviewers compare notes.")}],projectDeepDive:[{title:"The 2-minute version first",body:e("'Walk me through your current project' is almost always asked, often in the first 10 minutes. Start with a crisp 2-minute overview, then let the interviewer choose where to go deeper. Structure:","","- **Business purpose** (1-2 sentences): who uses the data and what decision it drives. 'Our pipelines feed the daily seller-payout and finance reporting for about 40,000 sellers.'","- **Scale** (1 sentence): sources, volume, frequency. 'About 1.2 TB of new data a day from 35 Postgres tables, Kafka order events and partner SFTP files.'","- **Architecture in one breath**: 'CDC with DMS into S3 raw, Spark on EMR to build bronze, silver and gold Iceberg tables, Redshift for BI, all orchestrated by Airflow on MWAA.'","- **Your role** (1-2 sentences): what you own. 'I own the orders and payments domain: 14 pipelines and the data-quality framework.'","- **One headline win**: 'The thing I am proudest of is cutting the daily batch from 5 hours to 70 minutes so finance gets numbers by 7 AM.'","","Then stop and ask: 'Shall I go deeper into the architecture, or into that optimisation?' That shows control and lets them steer.")},{title:"The architecture walkthrough",body:e("When they say 'go deeper', walk left to right through the data flow, and draw it if there is a whiteboard or shared screen. For each layer cover what, how much, how often and why:","","- **Sources**: which systems, formats, how data arrives (CDC, API, files, events), volume per day, peak versus average.","- **Ingestion**: tool (DMS, Debezium, Kafka, Firehose, Glue, custom), batch or streaming, how you handle schema changes and late data.","- **Storage**: S3 layout, table format (Parquet, Delta, Iceberg, Hudi), partitioning, file sizes, retention.","- **Processing**: Spark on EMR or Glue, dbt, SQL; cluster size, runtime, the expensive jobs and why.","- **Serving**: Redshift, Snowflake, Athena, Druid, APIs; who queries it and how fast it must be.","- **Orchestration and quality**: Airflow or Step Functions, retries, idempotency, data-quality checks, alerting, on-call.","","At every box, be ready for **'why this and not that?'**: why Iceberg and not plain Parquet, why EMR and not Glue, why batch and not streaming. A good answer names the trade-off: 'We needed MERGE for CDC deletes, and Iceberg gave us that on Athena and Spark without locking into one engine.' 'It was already there' is acceptable if you then say what you would choose today and why.")},{title:"The numbers to know cold",body:e("Interviewers test whether you really worked on a system by asking for numbers. Write these down and memorise them:","","- Daily and peak data volume (GB/TB per day, events per second at peak)","- Number of sources, tables and pipelines you own","- Biggest table: rows, size, partitioning","- SLAs: when data must land, end-to-end latency, how often you miss","- Cluster or job size: node type and count, DPUs, runtime of the main jobs","- Monthly cost of your pipelines, roughly, and the biggest line item","- Team size and your role in it","- Before-and-after numbers for every win you mention","","Approximate is fine ('around 800 GB a day, peaking at 3x during sale days'). Saying 'I am not sure, a lot' is not. If something is confidential, give an order of magnitude and say so.")},{title:"The deep-dive questions to prepare for",body:e("After the walkthrough, interviewers pick one box and drill. Prepare an answer for each:","","- How do you handle late-arriving or duplicate data?","- What happens if this job fails halfway? Is it idempotent? How do you backfill a month?","- How do you handle schema changes from upstream?","- How do you know the data is correct? What checks run, and who gets alerted?","- What was the last incident, and how did you find the root cause?","- What breaks first if volume grows 10x?","- How do you control cost?","","If you do not know an answer because another team owns that part, say so and explain how it works at the boundary with your part. Guessing is far worse than 'that is owned by the platform team; on my side I see it as...'.")},{title:"Handling 'what would you do differently?'",body:e("This question tests judgement and honesty, not whether your project was perfect. 'Nothing, it was well designed' is a red flag. A strong answer has three parts:","","- **The specific weakness**: 'We built on daily full snapshots of the orders table, so storage and runtime grow with history, not with change.'","- **Why it happened**: a reasonable constraint at the time, not blame. 'It was the fastest way to hit the launch date and volume was small then.'","- **What you would do now, with the trade-off**: 'I would use CDC with MERGE into Iceberg. It costs more engineering up front and needs compaction, but runtime becomes proportional to daily change.'","","Bonus points if you have already started fixing it or wrote the proposal. That turns a weakness into an ownership story.")}],mistakes:[{mistake:"Spending most of the answer on background and the team's project",instead:"Keep Situation and Task to 3-4 sentences together and spend over half your time on your own Actions."},{mistake:"Saying 'we' throughout, so the interviewer cannot tell what you did",instead:"Say 'I' for your work and name your exact part. Use 'we' only for context, then say 'my part was...'."},{mistake:"Results with no numbers: 'it became much faster' or 'stakeholders were happy'",instead:"Quote before and after: runtime, cost, freshness, incidents, adoption. Approximate is fine if you can explain how you measured it."},{mistake:"Telling a failure story where the failure was someone else's, or was not really a failure",instead:"Pick a real mistake you made, own it plainly, then show the fix, the prevention (tests, alerts, process) and what you do differently now."},{mistake:"Conflict stories where you were simply right and the other person was difficult",instead:"Show that you understood their goal, used data to disagree, found common ground or committed once a decision was made, and kept the relationship healthy."},{mistake:"Inventing or inflating stories, or claiming the whole team's numbers",instead:"Use real stories. Interviewers ask 4-5 levels of follow-up ('what was the partition key?', 'who disagreed?') and invented details collapse quickly."},{mistake:"Not knowing the basic numbers of your own project (volume, SLA, cluster size, cost)",instead:"Write a one-page cheat sheet of your project's numbers and memorise it before every loop."},{mistake:"Answering a different question than the one asked, or using one story for everything",instead:"Listen for the principle being probed (ownership, dive deep, disagreement) and pick the story that best fits it. Prepare 6-8 stories so you have choices."},{mistake:"Rambling for 6-8 minutes without checking in",instead:"Aim for 2-3 minutes, then stop. Offer to go deeper: 'I can explain how I debugged the skew if that is useful.'"},{mistake:"Being negative about a previous employer, manager or teammates",instead:"Describe constraints and disagreements neutrally and focus on what you did. Interviewers assume you will talk about them the same way."}],askThem:["What does a typical week look like for a data engineer on this team? How much is new building versus maintaining and on-call?","What are the biggest data problems the team needs to solve in the next 6-12 months?","How is the data platform organised: does this team own ingestion, modelling and serving end to end, or work with a central platform team?","How do you measure success for this role after the first 6 months?","What does on-call look like, and what was the last significant data incident the team handled?","How do data engineers here work with analysts, data scientists and product teams? Who decides what gets built?","What is the biggest piece of tech debt in the current stack, and is there appetite to fix it?","How do engineers grow here: what has the path from this level to the next looked like for people on your team?"]},r=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:n},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"}));export{r as g,s as p,i as q};
